<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Payerne</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Payerne is a town in the canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaud">Vaud</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, known for its large Romanesque abbey church. The priory was founded in the tenth century by Queen Bertha of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, widow of Rudolf II, and her daughter the empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide">Adelaide</a>, and was given to Cluny. In 1033 the emperor Conrad II was crowned king of Burgundy there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/payerne.jpg" alt="Payerne" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roland Zumbühl (Picswiss), Arlesheim (Commons:Picswiss project) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Picswiss_VD-49-06.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
